  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Sharing an apartment with a dog or cat is one of the best parts of coming home. It also means fitting a bed, food bowls, toys, and possibly a litter box into a space that may already feel tight. A dedicated pet corner keeps everything organized, gives your pet a place that feels like theirs, and helps protect your security deposit. Here is how to set one up, along with what to know about pet policies and fees when renting in West Carrollton.</p>

<h2>Start with your lease and pet policy</h2>
<p>Before you bring a pet home, or before you sign a lease with one, read the pet policy closely. Common terms include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Allowed animals:</strong> some properties allow cats only, some allow dogs and cats, and some allow small caged animals.</li>
<li><strong>Breed and weight limits:</strong> many communities restrict certain breeds or set a maximum weight for dogs.</li>
<li><strong>Number of pets:</strong> often capped at one or two per unit.</li>
<li><strong>Pet deposit:</strong> a one-time amount held against pet damage, which may or may not be refundable.</li>
<li><strong>Pet fee:</strong> a one-time, non-refundable charge some properties use instead of or in addition to a deposit.</li>
<li><strong>Pet rent:</strong> a monthly charge added to your rent.</li>
<li><strong>Vaccination and registration:</strong> proof of rabies vaccination and, for dogs, a county license is commonly required. Montgomery County requires dog licenses.</li>
</ul>
<p>Add pet costs to your monthly budget before you sign. Our <a href="/tools/rent-affordability-calculator/">rent affordability calculator</a> can help you see the real total. When you are ready to search, start with our list of <a href="/apartments/pet-friendly/">pet-friendly apartments in West Carrollton</a>.</p>

<h3>Assistance animals are different</h3>
<p>Under federal fair housing rules, service animals and emotional support animals are generally treated as assistance animals rather than pets. Housing providers typically cannot charge pet rent or pet deposits for them, and breed or weight restrictions usually do not apply, although tenants are still responsible for damage. This is not legal advice. If you have questions, ask the property about its process or contact a fair housing organization.</p>

<h2>Choosing the right spot for a pet corner</h2>
<p>The best pet corner is somewhere your pet already likes to be. Watch where your dog naps or where your cat tends to hang out. A few guidelines:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Choose a low-traffic area</strong> so your pet can rest without being stepped around.</li>
<li><strong>Stay near the family.</strong> Most pets prefer being close to where people spend time rather than isolated in a back room.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid drafts and direct heat.</strong> Keep beds away from exterior doors in winter and from heating vents that blow directly on them.</li>
<li><strong>Use hard floors for food and water</strong> when possible, since spills are easier to clean than on carpet.</li>
<li><strong>Keep litter boxes separate</strong> from food and water. Cats do not like to eat near where they go.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to include in your pet corner</h2>
<h3>A comfortable bed</h3>
<p>Pick a bed sized for your pet with a washable cover. For older dogs, an orthopedic bed is easier on joints. A crate covered with a blanket can also double as a cozy den for dogs who are crate trained.</p>

<h3>A feeding station</h3>
<p>Put bowls on a waterproof mat to catch spills and drips. Raised bowls can help larger dogs. Store food in an airtight container nearby to keep it fresh and to keep out pests, which matters in apartment buildings.</p>

<h3>Toy storage</h3>
<p>A small basket or bin keeps toys contained. Rotating a few toys at a time keeps them interesting and cuts clutter.</p>

<h3>Supplies within reach</h3>
<p>Hooks or a small shelf near the door can hold leashes, harnesses, poop bags, and a towel for muddy paws. In winter, that towel is essential for wiping off salt and slush after walks, which also protects your floors and your pet's paws.</p>

<!--products-->

<h2>Pet corners for small apartments</h2>
<p>In a studio or one-bedroom, every square foot counts. Try these space savers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Furniture that hides pet gear,</strong> like a litter box cabinet or an end table that doubles as a crate.</li>
<li><strong>Wall-mounted cat shelves</strong> give cats vertical territory without taking up floor space. Ask your landlord before drilling, and see our tips on <a href="/blog/small-apartment-storage-ideas/">small apartment storage</a> for no-drill options.</li>
<li><strong>Window perches</strong> that attach with suction cups give cats a view without hardware.</li>
<li><strong>Under-bed bins</strong> for extra pet food, treats, and supplies.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Protecting your deposit</h2>
<p>Pets and rentals can coexist without a big bill at move-out. A few habits help:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Document the unit at move-in</strong> with photos and notes, so pre-existing scratches or stains are not blamed on your pet.</li>
<li><strong>Clean accidents right away</strong> with an enzyme cleaner, which breaks down odors instead of masking them.</li>
<li><strong>Trim nails regularly</strong> to reduce scratches on floors and doors.</li>
<li><strong>Provide scratching posts</strong> for cats so they leave furniture and carpet alone.</li>
<li><strong>Vacuum often</strong> to keep fur from building up in carpet.</li>
</ol>
<p>Ohio Revised Code 5321.16 requires landlords to provide an itemized list of any deductions from your deposit within 30 days after the lease ends and you return possession. Normal wear and tear should not be deducted, but pet damage beyond that may be. This is not legal advice.</p>

<h2>Being a good pet neighbor</h2>
<p>Apartment living means shared walls, hallways, and green spaces. Pick up after your dog every time, keep your pet leashed in common areas, and work on barking if your dog is anxious when left alone. Puzzle toys, a walk before you leave, and gradual training can all help. If you live near the Great Miami River, the paved trails make for good long walks when the weather cooperates.</p>
<p>With a thoughtful setup and a pet policy you understand, your apartment can be a comfortable home for both of you.</p>
